<details><summary>Reveal the fixed-point and independence argument</summary>
For a represented unprovability predicate, diagonalization yields $T(G_n)\leftrightarrow\neg P(G_n)$. If $G_n$ were provable, soundness would make it true, and the equivalence would deny its provability. Hence it is unprovable and therefore true. A proof of its object-language negation would, by soundness, contradict its truth. Prescribed ranks satisfy $r(G_n)=n$, so equality $G_m=G_n$ forces $m=n$.
</details>

