<details><summary>Reveal the transport proof</summary>
Truth preservation gives $T_R(I(s))$ from $T_S(s)$. If $I(s)$ had a proof in the target, proof reflection would yield a proof of $s$ in the source, contradicting its assumed unprovability. Applying this once in each direction yields all four witnesses in the two-level tangle.
</details>

## Where to go next

The abstract results invite concrete arithmetic syntax, derived rank separation, weaker consistency assumptions, derivability logic, and graph-shaped networks of interpretations. The key discipline remains constant: distinguish represented predicates from arbitrary semantic properties, and distinguish transported truth from transported proof.
